Q: Is this bad coding practice? I feel like there is repetition and redundancy A: <p>There certainly are problems with your code. Not going into the details and apart from your code not compiling because a few things are missing in the question, I see (at least):</p>
<ol>
<li>All your functions should have only 1 variable.<br/>You seem to believe you need to add an extra parameter for the value they return but that is not the case.</li>
<li>Naming variables with obscure names such as <code>x</code> is bad practice. Same goes for the functions.<br/>The only reasons why your snippet is clear is because it fits in 1 file, is very short and comes with an introductory paragraph. Imagine what it would be in a code base of 10000+ lines maintained for several years by a team of 5 with turnover...</li>
<li>Using magic variables (<code>273.15</code>, <code>1.8</code>, <code>32</code>) is also bad practice (in general).<br/>The only reason why it may be accepted here is if we solve point 2.</li>
<li>It looks like you do <code>using namespace std;</code> (though absent from the snippet).<br/>Doing so with <code>std</code> is strongly discouraged. There are several old questions on SO that you can consult to learn why.<br/>BTW, comments are not a solution to unclear code. For one, your IDE won't use them for autocompletion. Never use comments if you can make your code explicit instead.</li>
<li><code>endl</code> creates an end line AND flushes the output sequence.<br/>That seems unnecessary. Use <code>'\n'</code> instead.</li>
<li><code>userNum</code> has no reason to be declared as <code>int</code>. It should be a <code>double</code> as it is passed into a function that accepts <code>double</code> as its parameter.</li>
<li>You do not check your user input.<br/>The unit is somewhat fine as the subsequent <code>switch</code> will ignore anything that is not a, b or c but there are better approaches if e.g. you want users to retry.<br/>Optionally, you may want to check users do not input temperatures below absolute 0.</li>
<li>As you thought, the code can be simplified.</li>
</ol>
<p>The below snippet introduces a specific namespace to group the <code>Temperature</code> functionality in 1 block, a <code>Unit</code> type that will greatly simplify the code for the conversion and it uses arrays to shorten the code required for output.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
namespace Temperature {
    enum class Unit { Kelvin, Celsius, Fahrenheit };
    double convert(double temperature, Unit from, Unit to) {
        // Convert from one unit to another using Kelvin as the pivot
        switch (from) {
            case Unit::Celsius: temperature = temperature + 273.15; break;
            case Unit::Fahrenheit: temperature = (temperature + 459.67) / 1.8;
        }
        // Halfway through: temperature is in Kelvin here.
        switch (to) {
            case Unit::Celsius: temperature = temperature - 273.15; break;
            case Unit::Fahrenheit: temperature = temperature * 1.8 - 459.67;
        }
        return temperature;
    }
}

int main() {
    using Temperature::Unit;
    double inputTemp = 0.;
    unsigned short inputUnit = 0;

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Temperature Converter\n&quot;;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Input a temperature to convert:\n&quot;;
    std::cin &gt;&gt; inputTemp;
    do {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;What unit (Type 1, 2 or 3, for the unit you want):\n&quot;;
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;1 (Celsius)\n&quot; \
                     &quot;2 (Fahrenheit)\n&quot; \
                     &quot;3 (Kelvin)\n&quot;;
        std::cin &gt;&gt; inputUnit;
    } while (inputUnit == 0 || inputUnit &gt; 3);
    inputUnit -= 1;

    // Units in an order that matches the value of inputUnit
    std::array&lt;Unit, 3&gt;        units     { Unit::Celsius, Unit::Fahrenheit, Unit::Kelvin };
    std::array&lt;std::string, 3&gt; unitNames { &quot;Celsius&quot;, &quot;Fahrenheit&quot;, &quot;Kelvin&quot; };

    std::cout &lt;&lt; inputTemp &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; unitNames[inputUnit] &lt;&lt; &quot; converted to: \n&quot;;
    for (unsigned short  i = 0; i &lt; 3; ++i) {
        if (i != inputUnit)
            std::cout &lt;&lt; unitNames[i] &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; Temperature::convert(inputTemp, units[inputUnit], units[i]) &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
